  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Which of the following statements is true regarding the sequence ${a}_{n}=4n+1$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\nI. Its first term is $5$\n\nII. Its first term is twice the second term\n\nIII. Its last term is $41$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- The first term of the sequence is represented by ${a}_{1}.$ Using the rule ${a}_{n}=4n+1,$ we compute ${a}_{1}=4+1=5.$ Therefore, statement I is true.\n\n- The second term of the sequence is represented by ${a}_{2}.$ Using the rule ${a}_{n}=4n+1,$ we compute ${a}_{2}=8+1=9.$  The first term was ${a}_{1}=5,$ and the second term is ${a}_{2}=9,$ so the first term is not twice the second term. Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- The sequence is defined for $n\\ge 1,$ which means that there is no limit to how large $n$ can be. There is no last term because the sequence keeps going on forever. Therefore, statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true.",
